 "title": "Robert Greene on Never Violating Egos at Work",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "Author Robert Greene explains why you should never violate someone's ego or make them feel insecure at work. He stresses the importance of letting superiors share in the credit and glory to avoid future resentment and betrayal.",
 "key_points": [
  "Never violate the ego of your boss, colleagues, or subordinates or make them feel insecure.",
  "Making people feel insecure in a professional setting always comes at a price.",
  "Allow superiors to take some of the credit and glory for your work.",
  "Failing to share credit makes superiors suspect you think you are better than them, leading to resentment, opposition, and betrayal."
 ],
 "content": "Post Text:\nViolating someone's ego at work has a price. And you always pay it eventually. 🧠\nComment \"STRATEGY\" for the full episode 🎙️\n\nTranscript:\n\"The worst thing you can do for your boss, for your colleagues, for the people underneath you is to violate their ego, to make them feel insecure. You will pay a price for it. You got to let the person above you take some of the credit, take some of the glory. Otherwise, they're going to be wondering about you, they're going to be thinking that you think that you're better than them. They will resent you, they will work against you, they will betray you in the end.\"\n\nOn-Screen Text:\n- The worst thing\n- your boss\n- your colleagues\n- the people underneath you\n- ROBERT GREENE Author of The 48 Laws of Power\n- the person above you\n- you\n- take some of the credit\n- take some of the glory\n- OPEN RESIDENCY Full Conversation on YouTube\n- OPEN-RESIDENCY® PRESENTED BY AppLovin",
